@page "/treemap"

<PageTitle>Treemap · RioCharts for Blazor and .NET</PageTitle>

<h1>Treemap</h1>
<p class="lead">
    A treemap shows how a total splits into parts by area, when there are too many parts for a pie. Each item is a rectangle sized by its
    value, nested inside its group.
</p>

<Example Title="From paths" Code="@PathCode">
    <Description>
        <p>
            Give <code>TreemapSeries</code> each item's path from the outermost group as <code>XValue</code>, here
            <code>"Asia/India"</code>. A group's value is the sum of its items, and its name sits in a header when there is room. Colors and
            legend items follow the top-level groups; click one in the legend to leave it out. <code>GroupSmallItems</code> gathers the
            smallest countries of each continent into "Other". Hover a rectangle for its share of the continent and of the world.
        </p>
        <p>
            Click anywhere in a continent, even tiny Oceania, to drill into it: it fills the chart, its countries get their own colors,
            and the path bar above (<em>All › Asia</em>) leads back up. (When items take clicks themselves, with <code>OnPointClick</code>
            or <code>Selection="ChartSelectionMode.Points"</code>, a group's header or frame drills instead.)
        </p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart TItem="Country" Items="Countries" Height="420">
            <TreemapSeries Name="Population" XValue="@(c => $"{c.Continent}/{c.Name}")" YValue="c => c.Millions" Format="0 M"
                           GroupSmallItems="0.03" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="From a parent-child list" Code="@ParentCode">
    <Description>
        <p>
            With <code>ParentValue</code>, <code>XValue</code> is the item's name and the parent's name builds the path: folders on a disk,
            an organisation, a bill of materials. Names must then be unique. Only items with a value get a rectangle; a folder's own size is
            the sum of what is in it.
        </p>
        <p>
            Deep trees get cramped, so <code>VisibleLevels="2"</code> draws two levels at a time: the top folders and what is in them.
            <em>Photos</em> shows as one cell with its total; open <em>Home</em> and its years appear. The data keeps every level.
        </p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart TItem="Folder" Items="Folders" Height="360">
            <TreemapSeries Name="Disk usage" XValue="f => f.Name" ParentValue="f => f.Parent" YValue="f => f.Gigabytes" Format="0 GB"
                           VisibleLevels="2" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<h2>Good to know</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Zero and negative values are left out (the chart's screen reader text says how many); a treemap cannot show them as areas.</li>
    <li>A flat list (no separator in the paths) is a one-level treemap; each item gets its own color and the legend stays hidden.</li>
    <li>Names and values are shortened with "…" or hidden when a rectangle is too small; the tooltip always has them in full.</li>
    <li>Use <code>PathSeparator</code> when names contain "/", e.g. <code>PathSeparator=" > "</code>.</li>
    <li>Drill down from code with <code>DrillDownAsync("Asia")</code> (<code>null</code> for the whole tree), bind the group shown with
        <code>@@bind-TreemapPath</code> (e.g. to keep it in the URL), and handle <code>OnDrillDown</code> to follow clicks, e.g. to load more detail.</li>
    <li>Treemaps cannot be mixed with other series types, and only one treemap series is drawn.</li>
</ul>

<h2>In JSON definitions</h2>
<p>
    <code>"type": "treemap"</code> with <code>"path": ["continent", "country"]</code> (the groups' fields, outermost first) and the value as
    <code>y</code>; or a single <code>x</code> field that holds the path. Rows with the same path are added up.
</p>

@code {
    private const string PathCode = """
        <RioChart TItem="Country" Items="countries">
            <TreemapSeries Name="Population" XValue="@(c => $"{c.Continent}/{c.Name}")" YValue="c => c.Millions"
                           Format="0 M" GroupSmallItems="0.03" />
        </RioChart>

        @code {
            record Country(string Continent, string Name, double Millions);
        }
        """;

    private const string ParentCode = """
        <RioChart TItem="Folder" Items="folders">
            <TreemapSeries Name="Disk usage" XValue="f => f.Name" ParentValue="f => f.Parent" YValue="f => f.Gigabytes" Format="0 GB"
                           VisibleLevels="2" />
        </RioChart>

        @code {
            record Folder(string Name, string? Parent, double? Gigabytes); // Parent null: a top-level folder
        }
        """;
}
